Transmissivity was estimated from the constant-rate data with the Cooper and Jacob (1946) straight-line approximation of the Theis (1935) solution, in which drawdown plotted against the logarithm of time becomes linear once the dimensionless time parameter u is less than about 0.01; transmissivity is proportional to the pumping rate divided by the drawdown change per log cycle. The late-time points satisfying that criterion were selected iteratively. As a check, the Theis curve was matched to the full record by least squares, and where recovery measurements were recorded the residual drawdown was analyzed by the Theis recovery method. For a single-well test the storativity cannot be resolved independently of well losses and the effective well radius, so the storativity was held at the value adopted in the pre-drilling analysis and is reported as not determinable from the test. Step-drawdown data, where available, were analyzed by the Jacob (1947) method to separate the formation-loss and well-loss components and to estimate the well efficiency at the design rate (Kruseman and de Ridder, 1990).

